<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rabbi Akiva</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 50–135</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Akiva ben Joseph, Rabbi Akiba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rabbi Akiva (Akiva ben Joseph) was one of the leading Jewish sages of the early 2nd century CE, whose systematic teaching of oral law laid foundations for the Mishnah. Rabbinic tradition says he hailed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Simon bar Kokhba</a> as the Messiah. He was executed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> around 135 CE for continuing to teach the Torah, and is remembered as a martyr.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rabbi-akiva.jpg" alt="Rabbi Akiva" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akiva.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Rabbi Akiva</b>, in the 1st and 2nd centuries, forbade its use in popular celebrations, saying that whoever sang it in wine taverns as a vulgar song lost his share in the world to come, yet he defended it as the &quot;Holy of Holies&quot; of the Writings.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a></li><li><blockquote>Its leader, Simon bar Kosiba, was hailed as Bar Kokhba, 'son of the star', a messianic title reportedly endorsed by <b>Rabbi Akiva</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bar-kokhba-state">Bar Kokhba state</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lod, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. Died in Caesarea Maritima, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.97,34.89&amp;year=105" aria-label="Lod on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5448 1001 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rabbi Akiva"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5898 1271h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5898 1271h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.97,34.89&amp;year=105">What was here in 105 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Everything is foreseen, yet free will is given…&#8221;</p><footer>Sayings of the Fathers (Pirke Avot) 3:19 &#183; tr. Joseph I. Gorfinkle, 1913 &#183; as the Mishnah records his saying &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8547/pg8547-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Beloved is man, for he was created in the image (of God)…&#8221;</p><footer>Sayings of the Fathers (Pirke Avot) 3:18 &#183; tr. Joseph I. Gorfinkle, 1913 &#183; as the Mishnah records his saying &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8547/pg8547-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
